Transfer Learning Graph – Simple Explanation

This graph compares:

Dotted Line = With Transfer Learning
Solid Line = Without Transfer Learning (Training from Scratch)

Axes
X-axis: Training Time / Epochs
Y-axis: Performance (Accuracy)
1. Higher Start

With Transfer Learning, the model starts with pre-trained knowledge.

So, from the first epoch itself, accuracy is already higher.

✅ Better starting performance

Without Transfer Learning:

Model starts from zero knowledge.
Must learn everything from scratch.

❌ Lower starting performance

2. Higher Slope

The dotted line rises faster.

This means:

Learning is faster.
Accuracy improves quickly.

✅ Faster convergence

Without Transfer Learning:

Learning is slower.
Requires more epochs.

❌ Slower convergence

3. Higher Asymptote

The dotted line reaches a higher final performance level.

This means:

Better final accuracy.
Better generalization.

✅ Higher final performance

Without Transfer Learning:

May stop at lower accuracy.

❌ Lower final performance

Simple Conclusion
With Transfer Learning
Starts with higher accuracy.
Learns faster.
Needs less training time.
Achieves better final performance.
Without Transfer Learning
Starts from scratch.
Learns slowly.
Needs more training time.
Usually achieves lower performance.

==========


Transfer Learning uses a pre-trained model that has already learned useful features from a large dataset.
It starts with higher accuracy because the model already has prior knowledge.
The model learns faster and requires fewer training epochs.
It reduces training time and computational cost.
It often achieves better final performance than training a model from scratch.

==========.
